.SH A) OVERVIEW
some things show be done or prepared for the whole unit-test, that is the
purpose of PREP.  PREP can contain primarily header inclusions, global and
local veriables

.SH B) TECHNICAL
PREP is typlically kept in "unit_head.unit" for what is request everywhere
and a second PREP can be put into the unit-test itself.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
PREP has only two (2) fields.

   verb--- § ---description----------- §
   PREP    § unit test setup           §

.SH D) VERB
PREP is the verb and must have zero indentation.  this field is a maximum of
twelve (12) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.

   verb--- § ---description----------- §
   PREP    § ·                         §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing is almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of sixty-five (65) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

   verb--- § ---description----------- §
   PREP    § unit test setup           §

.SH F) INCLUDE
since unit tests are generated, the main, and potentially private, header files
must be included, the "incl" verb is how that is done.

   verb------ § ---description----------- § ---header--- §
   ···incl    § include public header     § yLOG.h       §
   ···incl    § include private header    § yLOG_priv.h  §

.SH G) GLOBALS
simply add global variable requests under PREP.

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms--------------------------------- §
   ···global   § lrg  § - - -  § char *p = "testing";                      §

while these are global, they are static to each unit test, meaning any
updates do not travel between files.

.SH H) OUTPUT
there is no specific output for the PREP, include, or global variables verbs.
